  segs: [
    { t: "sign", title: "The Rotterdam rune", text: "PCOS needs 2 of 3: oligo- or anovulation, clinical or biochemical hyperandrogenism, and polycystic ovaries (20 or more follicles per ovary or volume 10 mL or more on TVUS, or a raised AMH in adults). Exclude the mimics first: thyroid disease, hyperprolactinaemia, non-classic CAH, androgen-secreting tumour and Cushing's." },
    { t: "gate", q: { k: "mcq", q: "Which feature points away from PCOS and towards an androgen-secreting tumour?", opts: ["Rapid virilisation: deeper voice, clitoromegaly", "Acne since her teens", "Hirsutism developing over 2 years", "Weight gain with acanthosis"], a: 0, why: "PCOS gives slowly progressive hirsutism and acne. Rapid onset with virilisation (voice change, clitoromegaly, temporal balding, muscle bulk) or a very high testosterone suggests an ovarian or adrenal tumour: image the ovaries and adrenals." } },
    { t: "pick", title: "The Mimic Moths", prompt: "Collect the conditions you must exclude before calling it PCOS. Avoid the rest.", good: [["Thyroid", "Thyroid disease (TSH)"], ["Prolactin", "Hyperprolactinaemia (PRL)"], ["NCCAH", "Non-classic CAH (17-OH progesterone)"], ["Tumour", "Androgen-secreting tumour (testosterone, DHEAS)"], ["Cushing's", "Cushing's syndrome (if clinical features)"]], bad: [["Endometriosis", "Endometriosis", "Endometriosis causes pain, not hyperandrogenism or anovulation."], ["Asherman's", "Asherman's syndrome", "Intrauterine adhesions cause amenorrhoea but no androgen excess, and she has no history of curettage."], ["Dermoid", "Ovarian dermoid", "A dermoid doesn't secrete androgens or cause anovulation."]], why: "Each mimic has a simple test: TSH, prolactin, early-morning follicular 17-OH progesterone, testosterone and DHEAS, and screening for Cushing's when there are clinical features." },
    { t: "check" },
    { t: "gate", q: { k: "results", q: "Miss B's results are back. What do they show?", panel: [["FSH", "4", ""], ["LH", "16", "H"], ["Prolactin", "400 (0–600)", ""], ["Testosterone", "3.5 (0.2–2.8)", "H"], ["DHEAS", "13 (2–11)", "H"], ["TSH", "2.5", ""], ["Free androgen index", "12 (<5)", "H"], ["SHBG", "10 (20–200)", "L"], ["Ultrasound", "40+ follicles per ovary, endometrium 8 mm", ""]], opts: ["Biochemical hyperandrogenism with polycystic ovaries: PCOS", "Premature ovarian insufficiency", "Hyperprolactinaemia", "Hypothalamic amenorrhoea"], a: 0, why: "With her anovulation, she meets all three Rotterdam criteria. Low-normal FSH rules out POI and prolactin is normal. Hyperinsulinaemia suppresses SHBG, so free testosterone and FAI rise. A raised LH:FSH ratio is typical but isn't a diagnostic criterion. A mildly raised DHEAS is common in PCOS; a markedly raised one suggests an adrenal source." } },
    { t: "sign", title: "Shadows of the long road", text: "PCOS brings insulin resistance and type 2 diabetes, dyslipidaemia, cardiovascular risk, endometrial hyperplasia and cancer (anovulation leaves oestrogen unopposed), sleep apnoea, and depression and anxiety. Screen with an OGTT, lipids, BP and a mood check. Protect the endometrium with a COCP, cyclical progestogen or an LNG-IUS." },
    { t: "gate", q: { k: "multi", q: "Which screening does Miss B need for her long-term risks? Pick all that apply.", opts: ["Oral glucose tolerance test", "Fasting lipid profile", "Blood pressure", "Depression and anxiety screen", "CA-125", "Bone density scan"], a: [0, 1, 2, 3], why: "Screen the metabolic and mood complications: OGTT (repeat every 1–3 years depending on risk), lipids, BP and mood. CA-125 and bone density have no role in PCOS." } },
    { t: "gate", q: { k: "results", q: "Her further tests are back. What do you tell her?", panel: [["OGTT fasting", "4.0", ""], ["OGTT 1 h", "8.0", ""], ["OGTT 2 h", "6.0", ""], ["Cholesterol", "6.6 (<5.5)", "H"], ["LDL", "3.8 (<3.5)", "H"], ["HDL", "1.2", ""], ["Triglyceride", "1.5 (<2.5)", ""]], opts: ["Normal glucose tolerance with dyslipidaemia: lifestyle change, repeat lipids in 6–12 months", "Impaired glucose tolerance: start metformin", "Type 2 diabetes: refer to endocrinology", "All normal: no follow-up"], a: 0, why: "A 2-hour glucose under 7.8 is normal, so repeat the OGTT in 1–3 years. Cholesterol and LDL are raised: start with dietitian input and exercise, then recheck in 6–12 months." } },
    { t: "check" },
    { t: "sign", title: "Taming the hair", text: "Start cosmetic measures now (shaving, waxing, laser or electrolysis), because hormonal treatment is slow. Use a COCP, which also regulates cycles, plus spironolactone if needed. Spironolactone needs reliable contraception because it can feminise a male fetus. Judge the response after at least 6 months." },
    { t: "leap", q: { k: "mcq", q: "How long should she wait before judging hormonal treatment for hirsutism?", opts: ["At least 6 months", "2 weeks", "6 weeks", "2 years"], a: 0, why: "Hair follicles cycle slowly, so allow at least 6 months, and keep using cosmetic measures in the meantime." } },
    { t: "sign", title: "When she wants a child", text: "Letrozole is first line for ovulation induction in PCOS. If that fails, use low-dose step-up gonadotrophins or laparoscopic ovarian drilling. Lifestyle and weight loss help with all of them.", note: { said: "clomiphene citrate as first line", now: "letrozole first line (2023 international guideline). PPCOS II: live births 27.5% vs 19.1% with clomiphene." } },
    { t: "gate", q: { k: "mcq", q: "Years later Miss B wants to conceive and is anovulatory. What is first-line ovulation induction?", opts: ["Letrozole", "Clomiphene citrate", "IVF", "Laparoscopic ovarian drilling"], a: 0, why: "Letrozole gives higher ovulation and live-birth rates than clomiphene in PCOS (PPCOS II). Gonadotrophins or drilling come second line, and IVF after that.", note: { said: "clomiphene citrate", now: "letrozole" } } },
    { t: "sign", title: "The Consult Path", steps: [["History", "Focused history: cycles, androgen symptoms, weight, family history"], ["Examine", "Examine: BMI, BP, Ferriman-Gallwey, acanthosis, virilisation"], ["Bloods + US", "Bloods (FSH, LH, PRL, TSH, testosterone, SHBG, DHEAS) and pelvic US"], ["Explain", "Explain the diagnosis and long-term risks"], ["Plan", "Lifestyle, hirsutism treatment, endometrial protection, future fertility"]], text: "Typical OSCE marking runs history, examination, investigations, explanation, then management. Say each part out loud." }
  ]
